 First, we note that the equation is symmetric. This means that if $r$ is a solution, then its complex conjugate $\bar{r}$ is also a solution. This is a property of real polynomials with complex roots.
Then we can pair up the solutions to get $$r_1\bar{r_1}=r_2\bar{r_2}=r_3\bar{r_3}=r_4\bar{r_4}=r_5\bar{r_5}.$$
The reason we know this is because the solutions of the equation $x^{2n}+a_1x^{2n-1}+...+a_nx^2+a_{n-1}x+...+a_n=0$ come in pairs: the conjugate solutions have the same magnitude. This is because if $r$ is a solution of the equation, then so is $-r$. This is because if you have a polynomial $ax^2+bx+c=0$, its solutions are $$\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a},$$ and the term in the square root is supposed to be negative.
Now let's calculate the magnitude of the product $r\bar{r}$: $$|r\bar{r}|=\sqrt{Re(r)\cdot Re(\bar{r})+Im(r)\cdot Im(\bar{r})}\\ =\sqrt{Re(r)^2+Im(r)^2}\\ =|r|,$$ so all of the imaginaries have cancelled out and you are left with the magnitude of $r$ times the magnitude of $\bar{r}$. Remember that the magnitudes of the solutions are symmetric, so we end up with the magnitude of some complex number squared equally on both sides of the equation. Thus, we get $$|r_1|^2+|r_2|^2=|r_1|^4,$$
and similarly for the rest.
Finally, we get $$\frac 1{r_1\bar{r}_1}+\frac 1{r_2\bar{r}_2}+\frac 1{r_3\bar{r}_3}+\frac 1{r_4\bar{r}_4}+\frac 1{r_5\bar{r}_5} =+\frac 1{|r_1|^2}+\frac4{|r_1|^4}.$$

